---
import Charter from "../../layouts/Charter.astro";
import Article from "../../components/Article.astro";
import { DISCORD_INVITE } from "../../config";

const articles = [
  { id: "short-version", title: "The short version" },
  { id: "discord-sign-in", title: "Signing in with Discord" },
  { id: "battle-net", title: "Linking Battle.net" },
  { id: "events", title: "Event sign-ups" },
  { id: "hall-of-heroes", title: "Hall of Heroes" },
  { id: "cookies", title: "Cookies" },
  { id: "bot", title: "The guild bot" },
  { id: "who-else", title: "Who else handles it" },
  { id: "deleting", title: "Deleting your data" },
  { id: "changes", title: "Changes" },
];
---

<Charter
  title="Privacy · SideQuest"
  description="What SideQuest's site and Discord bot keep about you, and how to delete it."
  heading="PRIVACY"
  lede="What this site and the guild's Discord bot keep about you, who else touches it, and how to make it go away."
  effective="October 29, 2026"
  artPosition="12% 50%"
  articles={articles}
>
  <Article id="short-version" n={1} title="The short version">
    <ul>
      <li>We keep only what we need to run the guild: who you are on Discord, and your event sign-ups.</li>
      <li>No ads, no analytics, no trackers. We never sell or share your data with anyone for marketing.</li>
      <li>
        Unlinking Battle.net erases your Battle.net data. Deleting your account erases your site account, every event
        sign-up, and your Discord chat XP. Both are on your <a href="/account/">Account</a> page and take effect
        immediately.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>"We" means SideQuest's officers, who run this site for the guild.</p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="discord-sign-in" n={2} title="Signing in with Discord">
    <p>
      Signing in asks Discord for two permissions: who you are, and your membership in the SideQuest server. From those
      we keep:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your Discord ID, username, display name and avatar</li>
      <li>Your nickname and roles in the SideQuest server, and whether you're still in it</li>
      <li>When you last signed in</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Your username, display name, avatar, nickname, roles and guild membership stay in sync with Discord continuously,
      not just when you sign in — our bot watches for changes in the SideQuest server and updates them automatically.
      When you last signed in only updates at sign-in. We never see your email, password or messages, and we don't keep
      the token Discord hands us. It's used once during sign-in and then thrown away.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a guild master confirms you're in the in-game guild, we also keep that and when it happened. It's what opens
      the member channels in Discord, and a guild master can remove it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first time you sign in, our bot posts a notice in an officers-only Discord channel with your name, username,
      avatar and BattleTag (if you've linked one), so a guild master can confirm you. Clicking Approve there does the
      same thing as confirming you on this site; the notice is updated either way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Guild masters can see a members' list on this site with your Discord roles, sign-in and link dates, your event
      sign-ups and comments, and any moderation records. When an officer creates, changes or deletes an event or addon,
      or changes the roster, we keep a record of which officer did it and when. If an officer deletes their account,
      those records keep only their display name.
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="battle-net" n={3} title="Linking Battle.net">
    <p>
      Linking is optional. It asks Battle.net only for your account ID and BattleTag, and we keep those two plus when
      you linked. Not your characters, email or real name, and no token is kept.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Unlinking erases all three right away.</strong>
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="events" n={4} title="Event sign-ups">
    <p>
      When you sign up for an event we keep your answer (going, maybe or can't), and when you give them, your character,
      class, role and note. On raid nights officers may also place you in a group or on the bench. You can sign up
      either on this site or with the Going/Maybe/Can't buttons on the event's post in Discord; both write the same
      sign-up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Event details and sign-ups are for signed-in members:</strong> the public can see an event's name, kind,
      time and place, but who's going, the plan (notes), the raid chart and the roster are only visible once you sign in
      with Discord. Don't put anything in a note you wouldn't say in guild chat.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each event also has a post in the Discord <strong>#events</strong> forum, where the bot notes when someone signs
      up, changes their answer, adds a comment or withdraws. It mentions them by their Discord account (without a
      notification) and repeats their sign-up comment there.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you click <strong>Interested</strong> on an event in Discord, we keep your Discord ID against that event.
      Signed-in members can see the count and the names of people who have signed in on this site; the public only sees
      the event's name, kind, time and place.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also create a private calendar link on your <a href="/account/">Account</a> page: a random secret URL for
      Google Calendar or Apple Calendar. Anyone holding that link can see the guild's events and your sign-ups, so don't
      share it; making a new link stops the old one working right away.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can mark date ranges you'll be away on the same page. We keep them until they pass, show them to signed-in
      members on an event's page for that date, and the bot gives you the <strong>Away on a Quest</strong> Discord role
      while a range is active.
    </p>
    <p>
      After an event, officers record who actually came: a Came or No-show mark for each sign-up, plus anyone who showed
      up without signing up first (a walk-in). While an event is running, the bot notes how many minutes each member
      spends in the guild's voice channels during that event — minutes only, nothing about what's said — as a hint
      officers can use when marking attendance. Officers and GMs can see this roster on the Officers page; GMs can also
      see an aggregate attendance report and trend chart across the guild. Attendance feeds your
      <a href="#hall-of-heroes">Hall of Heroes</a> stats, EP and badges.
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="hall-of-heroes" n={5} title="Hall of Heroes">
    <p>
      The <a href="/heroes/">Hall of Heroes</a> is a leaderboard for signed-in guild members only: your events and raids
      attended, EP, attendance rate and chat level are shown there next to every other member's, ranked against each
      other for the current season, the last month, and all time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Badges on the Hall of Heroes (First Steps, Raid Regular and the rest) are earned automatically from the same
      attendance and chat data, and are also Discord roles: the bot creates and assigns them, and announces new ones in
      the server. Anyone in the server can see the badge roles on your Discord profile; signed-in members can also see
      badge names and the date each was earned on your Hall of Heroes profile.
    </p>
    <p>
      Deleting your account (see <a href="#deleting">Deleting your data</a>) removes you from the Hall of Heroes and
      strips every badge role the bot can manage, the same as any other Discord role it granted you.
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="cookies" n={6} title="Cookies">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>sq_session</strong> keeps you signed in. It's encrypted, holds only your account number, can't be read
        by scripts, and lasts 30 days or until you sign out.
      </li>
      <li>
        During a Discord or Battle.net sign-in, the same cookie briefly holds a one-time code that stops forged
        sign-ins.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you're an officer, your browser's local storage keeps one flag, <strong>sq-officer</strong>, so the Officers
        link shows without a flicker. It's checked against your account on every page and removed once you're signed out
        or no longer an officer.
      </li>
      <li>
        To load instantly, your browser keeps a copy of this site's pages and pictures, and of the events and addons you
        last looked at, including the sign-ups shown on them. Your account page and the officers' pages are never
        copied. The event and addon copies are deleted whenever you sign in, sign out, sign up or edit anything, and
        clearing this site's data in your browser removes all of it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>No analytics or advertising cookies. If you never sign in, you get no cookies at all.</p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="bot" n={7} title="The guild bot">
    <p>Inside the SideQuest Discord, the guild's bot keeps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your chat XP and level, erased if you delete your site account</li>
      <li>
        Moderation records (warnings, timeouts, kicks and bans, including the bot's own automatic ones), with the reason
        and which officer acted
      </li>
      <li>
        A log channel only officers can read, where the bot copies edited and deleted messages, joins and leaves, and
        nickname and role changes
      </li>
      <li>How many people are online and in each voice channel, as numbers only, never names</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These belong to your Discord account in the server, not your site account, except chat XP: deleting your site
      account erases it right away. Moderation records are kept to keep the guild safe, and guild masters can read them,
      and correct a reason, on this site.
    </p>
    <p>
      Separately, once you're signed in, the site also shows which SideQuest members are currently online in Discord —
      name, status and avatar — pulled live from Discord's own public server widget, not stored. Avatars load directly
      from Discord's CDN. Signed-out visitors only see a total online count, never names.
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="who-else" n={8} title="Who else handles it">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Discord</strong> and <strong>Blizzard (Battle.net)</strong> run the sign-ins, under their own privacy
        policies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> sits in front of the site, and <strong>Cabotage</strong> hosts it on Amazon Web
        Services in the US. Like any website, they see standard request details such as your IP address in order to
        deliver and protect it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>That's everyone. Nothing else is shared.</p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="deleting" n={9} title="Deleting your data">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Unlink Battle.net</strong> on your <a href="/account/">Account</a> page: your Battle.net data is erased.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Delete my account</strong> on the same page: your site account, every event sign-up and attendance mark,
        your voice-minute records, your calendar link, your away dates, and your Discord chat XP are erased, and you're
        signed out. Moderation records stay. The bot strips every Discord role it can manage from you within a minute
        and puts you back behind #sign-the-ledger, as if you'd just joined.
      </li>
      <li>
        Removing SideQuest from <em>Authorized Apps</em> in Discord only stops future sign-ins. It doesn't reach us, so
        use Delete my account to erase what we hold.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For anything else, message an officer in the <a href={DISCORD_INVITE}>SideQuest Discord</a>.
    </p>
  </Article>

  <Article id="changes" n={10} title="Changes">
    <p>
      If what we keep changes, this page and its date change with it, and anything significant gets announced in the
      Discord first.
    </p>
  </Article>
</Charter>
